1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,400
Creational patterns asked how objects are
born. Structural patterns ask how objects
are assembled so you can extend behavior
without melting the type hierarchy.
Composition wraps behavior.

2
00:00:10,519 --> 00:00:24,280
Inheritance is not banned — but exploding
subclasses for every feature combination
is how codebases become museums of
LoggedCachedSecureX. Suppose you have a
repository. You need logging around saves.
Then metrics.

3
00:00:24,399 --> 00:00:38,200
Then a circuit breaker. Inheritance would
tempt LoggingRepo extends JdbcRepo, then
MetricsLoggingRepo extends LoggingRepo. A
decorator adds behavior by wrapping the
same interface: Look at this java for a
moment.

4
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:44,520
LoggingRepo is still a Repo. Callers need
not know whether they hold the raw
repository or a wrapped one.

5
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:52,520
Decorator versus inheritance, asked in an
interview, is exactly this: add behavior
via composition without exploding
subclasses.

6
00:00:52,640 --> 00:01:05,040
Stack decorators carefully — five wrappers
with unclear order recreate the complexity
you fled. Order matters: authentication
before rate limiting before caching is a
different system than the reverse.

7
00:01:05,159 --> 00:01:16,719
Document the stack. Adapters appear when
two interfaces do not match. The SMS
provider wants sendSms(Phone, Text). Your
domain port says notify(UserId,
Notification).

8
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:31,840
The adapter loads the phone number,
formats the text, translates errors into
domain failures, and isolates the SDK.
When the vendor changes, one class moves.
Without an adapter, vendor types leak into
services and tests.

9
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:45,200
Facades simplify subsystems: one entry
point that orchestrates collaborating
classes so callers are not wired to every
moving part. They fail in two opposite
ways. Too thin: pass-through methods with
no policy.

10
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:57,600
Too thick: a god object that knows the
entire company. A good facade encodes a
few use cases your system actually owns —
"charge customer for invoice" — not one
hundred low-level dials.

11
00:01:57,719 --> 00:02:10,800
Facades and adapters often travel
together: facade for your policy, adapter
for their shape. Split when the vendor
story and the business story stop changing
for the same reasons. Proxies control
access.

12
00:02:10,919 --> 00:02:18,879
They stand in for a real object and
intercept calls — lazy loading, security,
remote access, transactional boundaries.

13
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:26,520
If you have used Spring AOP, you have
lived with proxies: the bean you inject
may wrap your class to apply transactions
or security.

14
00:02:26,639 --> 00:02:33,239
Sharp edges: equals, final methods, and
self-invocation (this.method() bypassing
the proxy).

15
00:02:33,359 --> 00:02:44,799
A method annotated transactional calls
another method on this — the call does not
pass through the proxy, so the annotation
does not apply. Structural understanding
prevents "Spring is broken" tickets.

16
00:02:44,919 --> 00:02:57,359
Walk a design conversation. "Should we
facade the payment SDK?" If three services
each duplicate the same five SDK calls,
yes. If one call site exists, maybe
premature.

17
00:02:57,479 --> 00:03:13,159
"Should we decorate the repository with
caching?" Only if cache policy is clear —
keys, TTL, invalidation — otherwise you
hide a correctness problem behind a
structural pattern. Patterns amplify both
good and bad policy.

18
00:03:13,280 --> 00:03:25,840
When reviewing wrappers, ask: does this
buy a policy we can name? If the name is
fuzzy, you may be looking at accidental
complexity. Composition keeps showing up
as the safe default. Adapter for foreign
shapes.

19
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:35,280
Decorator for layered policies on one
interface. Facade for a curated subsystem
door. Proxy for controlled access and
infrastructure concerns.

20
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:41,319
Spring's AOP proxies are not a different
universe; they are this idea with a
framework engine.

21
00:03:41,439 --> 00:03:52,520
Before you leave structure, notice how
these appear in reviews without the names:
"wrap this client to add retries" is
decorator talk; "hide these five SDK calls
behind one method" is facade talk;

22
00:03:52,639 --> 00:03:56,319
"translate this third-party DTO at the
boundary" is adapter talk.

23
00:03:56,439 --> 00:04:07,240
When structural assembly is in place, the
next question is how objects communicate
and how algorithms vary without rewriting
callers — Strategy, Observer, Command, and
friends.
